issues.body:
### What happened?

Parallel edits through two hard links can both succeed while one change is lost. The queue uses `realpath`, which returns different paths for hard links to the same file. I reproduced this on macOS arm64 against main `58c0b02ce552` (1.4.0), calling the built-in tools directly without extensions: `alpha\nbeta\n` became `ALPHA\nbeta\n` instead of `ALPHA\nBETA\n`.

### Steps to reproduce

From a source checkout, save this as `repro.mjs` in the repository root and run `node repro.mjs`:

```js
import { link, mkdtemp, rm, writeFile } from "node:fs/promises";
import { tmpdir } from "node:os";
import { join } from "node:path";
import { withFileMutationQueue as queue } from "./packages/coding-agent/src/core/tools/file-mutation-queue.ts";
const dir = await mkdtemp(join(tmpdir(), "pi-hardlink-"));
try {
  const a = join(dir, "a"), b = join(dir, "b"), order = [];
  await writeFile(a, "hello"); await link(a, b);
  let release;
  const held = new Promise(resolve => { release = resolve; });
  const first = queue(a, async () => { order.push("a:start"); await held; order.push("a:end"); });
  const second = queue(b, async () => { order.push("b:start"); });
  await queue(join(dir, "unrelated"), async () => {});
  release(); await Promise.all([first, second]);
  console.log(order);
} finally { await rm(dir, { recursive: true, force: true }); }
```

Actual: `a:start, b:start, a:end`. Expected: `a:start, a:end, b:start`. The unrelated call witnesses FIFO registration; no timing sleeps are needed.

### Expected behavior

Serialize mutations of the same existing file across hard links, while keeping different files parallel. A [file-identity fix with regression tests](https://github.com/liangzhiyu/pi/compare/main...fix/hardlink-file-mutation-ordering) is prepared: `npm run check` and 96 tool tests pass; `./test.sh` is still blocked by missing package build artifacts and the Rust daemon. May I submit a PR after completing those prerequisites? This is separate from missing-file directory symlinks in #9389.

### Version

1.4.0 / main `58c0b02ce552`, Node 24.19.0, macOS arm64.

### What happened?

An existing conversation failed with HTTP 400 because `input[472].id` contained `/` (a `/1` suffix). The upstream accepts only letters, numbers, underscores and dashes. The original ID's source is not yet established.

Separately, the offline reproduction below confirms that Pi replays a synthetic invalid assistant-message ID unchanged. `convertResponsesMessages()` checks missing/overlong text-signature IDs, but not their characters. This can leave subsequent requests carrying the same invalid ID. Related: #1022 (tool IDs), #7883 (message-ID prefixes).

### Steps to reproduce

Run with `@earendil-works/pi-ai@1.3.0` installed, using `node --input-type=module` (no network, credentials, CLI or extensions loaded):

```js
import { convertResponsesMessages } from '@earendil-works/pi-ai/api/openai-responses-shared';
const model = { id: 'fixture', provider: 'openai', api: 'openai-responses', reasoning: false, input: ['text'] };
const messages = [{
  role: 'assistant', provider: model.provider, api: model.api, model: model.id,
  content: [{ type: 'text', text: 'hello',
    textSignature: JSON.stringify({ v: 1, id: 'msg_fixture/1' }) }],
  stopReason: 'stop', timestamp: 0,
}];
const input = convertResponsesMessages(model, { messages }, new Set(['openai']));
console.log(input[0].id); // msg_fixture/1
console.log(/^[A-Za-z0-9_-]+$/.test(input[0].id)); // false
```

### Expected behavior

Do not emit invalid message-level IDs when replaying existing history; preserve valid IDs and phase metadata. The same missing/length-only check remains in the v1.4.0 source (source inspection only, not a runtime test).

AI-assisted report; reproduction and verification details are disclosed in a follow-up comment.

### Version

@earendil-works/pi-ai 1.3.0 (offline reproduction); v1.4.0 source inspected

> AI disclosure: drafted with an AI coding assistant. I checked the cited code against 1.3.0 and ran the repro below.

### What happened?

`read`, `edit` and `write` take `operations` so an embedder controls file access, but two helpers still call `node:fs` directly, before any operation runs:

- `read`: `resolveReadPathAsync` → `pathExists` → `access` from `node:fs/promises` (`dist/core/tools/path-utils.js:72`, called from `read.js:73`).
- `edit` and `write`: `withFileMutationQueue` → `getMutationQueueKey` → `realpath` from `node:fs/promises` (`dist/core/tools/file-mutation-queue.js:14`).

My app runs pi in Electron, where `node:fs` is patched to treat `.asar` files as folders. I pass operations built on Electron's `original-fs`, which treats an `.asar` as a plain file. When the agent reads, edits or writes a path that is itself an `.asar` (common in Electron projects: `node_modules/electron/dist/resources/default_app.asar`), those two calls make Electron open the archive and keep the handle for the life of the process. On Windows the file then can't be renamed or deleted until the process exits (EBUSY), so packaging the project or deleting a folder holding it fails. Electron's patched `access` also reports the existing archive as missing (ENOENT), so `pathExists` returns false for it.

The same calls also reach the local disk when the operations target another machine (the setup in #5350).

### Steps to reproduce

Windows, a folder with `electron` and `@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent` installed, run `electron issue.mjs`. It copies Electron's own `default_app.asar` for each check, then has a second process rename the copy:

```js
import { app } from "electron";
import { spawnSync } from "node:child_process";
import * as path from "node:path";
import { createEditTool, createReadTool, createWriteTool, withFileMutationQueue } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

const ofs = process.getBuiltinModule("original-fs"); // Electron's fs that treats .asar as a plain file
const ops = {
  readFile: (p) => ofs.promises.readFile(p),
  writeFile: (p, c) => ofs.promises.writeFile(p, c),
  access: (p) => ofs.promises.access(p),
  mkdir: (p) => ofs.promises.mkdir(p, { recursive: true }).then(() => {}),
};
const source = path.join(path.dirname(process.execPath), "resources", "default_app.asar");

// A second process renames the file and back; EBUSY means this process still holds it open.
const held = (file) => spawnSync(process.execPath, ["-e",
  "const fs=require('fs'),f=process.argv[1];try{fs.renameSync(f,f+'.x');fs.renameSync(f+'.x',f);console.log('free')}catch(e){console.log(e.code)}", file],
  { env: { ...process.env, ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE: "1" }, encoding: "utf8" }).stdout.trim();

async function check(name, run) {
  const file = path.resolve(`${name}.asar`);
  ofs.copyFileSync(source, file);
  await run(file).catch(() => {});
  console.log(`${name.padEnd(22)} ${held(file)}`);
}

app.whenReady().then(async () => {
  const cwd = process.cwd();
  await check("original-fs-readFile", (f) => ofs.promises.readFile(f));
  await check("read-tool", (f) => createReadTool(cwd, { operations: ops }).execute("1", { path: f }));
  await check("write-tool", (f) => createWriteTool(cwd, { operations: ops }).execute("2", { path: f, content: "x" }));
  await check("edit-tool", (f) => createEditTool(cwd, { operations: ops }).execute("3", { path: f, edits: [{ oldText: "a", newText: "b" }] }));
  await check("withFileMutationQueue", (f) => withFileMutationQueue(f, async () => {}));
  app.exit(0);
});
```

Output (pi 1.3.0, Electron 44.9.0, Windows 11):

```
original-fs-readFile   free
read-tool              EBUSY
write-tool             EBUSY
edit-tool              EBUSY
withFileMutationQueue  EBUSY
```

### Expected behavior

With custom `operations`, the tools touch the file system only through them. For example, `ReadOperations` gets an optional `exists`, and `EditOperations` and `WriteOperations` an optional `realpath` that `withFileMutationQueue` uses for its key (falling back to `path.resolve` when custom operations leave it out). The defaults stay as they are.

### Version

1.3.0

## Summary

When one process cleanup returns `uncertain` — a first-class outcome in this codebase, not a theoretical one — the `throw` skips the release loop for **all** processes in that owner, including the ones whose cleanup already succeeded, and skips the owner deletion. The workspace operation counter stays permanently above zero, which permanently pins that workspace generation and its manifest tree against retention. Repeated turn churn then grows the workspace directory without bound.

## Location

`src/node-host/node-worker-workspace-processes.ts:261-288`

```ts
  private async stopOwners(owners: ProcessOwner[]): Promise<void> {
    for (const owner of owners) {
      owner.accepting = false;
    }
    const outcomes = await Promise.allSettled(
      owners.map(async (owner) => {
        const processOutcomes = await Promise.allSettled(
          [...owner.processes.values()].map((process) => process.cleanup()),
        );
        const errors = processOutcomes.flatMap((outcome) =>
          outcome.status === "rejected" ? [outcome.reason] : [],
        );
        if (errors.length) {
          throw new AggregateError(errors, "Workspace process cleanup failed");
        }
        for (const process of owner.processes.values()) {
          process.releaseWorkspace();
        }
        this.owners.delete(owner.key);
      }),
    );
```

## Mechanism

1. `process.cleanup` is the supervisor scope cleanup; `joinExtinction` (same file, `:246-256`) rejects with *"Workspace process cleanup is uncertain: ..."* whenever `run.waitForExtinction()` reports `uncertain`.
2. On any single rejection, the `throw` at line 274 skips the loop at 277 for **all** processes in that owner, and skips `this.owners.delete(owner.key)`.
3. `process.releaseWorkspace` is the release handle returned by `beginWorkspaceOperation` (`src/node-host/node-worker-workspace.ts:472-498`), which decrements `activeWorkspaceOperations[generationKey]`. Skipping it leaves the counter permanently above zero.
4. `currentLocalProtection` (`node-worker-workspace.ts:475-486`) folds every `activeWorkspaceOperations` key into the protected set, which is exactly what gates deletion in `collectRetainedWorkspaceSnapshot`: candidates are skipped via `if (!currentSnapshot.retainedGenerations.has(key) && !localProtection.has(key))`, and the `manifests`/`.openclaw-worker` teardown is gated on `!hasCurrentLocalProtection()`.
5. Nothing re-drives this automatically: `stopEnvironment`/`close` are the only entry points, the owner is still in `this.owners`, so a later call retries — but an idle node host never issues one, and there is no timer or GC path.

## Impact

One `uncertain` cleanup result permanently pins that workspace generation plus its manifest tree against retention, so repeated turn churn on the same environment grows `.../workspaces/<env>/<session>/` and `.../.openclaw-worker/manifests/` **without bound** — on a long-lived node host, that is disk exhaustion.

## Reproduction

1. Run two or more preview/background processes in one worker workspace.
2. Call `stopEnvironment` (turn end, `ownerEpoch` bump, or host close) while at least one scope cleanup cannot confirm descendant extinction.
3. Observe `activeWorkspaceOperations[generationKey]` stay above zero forever.

## Proposed fix

Release per-process regardless of siblings' failures — move the loop before the throw:

```ts
        const errors = processOutcomes.flatMap((outcome) =>
          outcome.status === "rejected" ? [outcome.reason] : [],
        );
        // Release EVERY process's workspace hold, even when a sibling failed: a
        // pinned generation blocks retention for the whole workspace and is a
        // disk-exhaustion path, whereas the failure is already surfaced.
        for (const process of owner.processes.values()) {
          try {
            process.releaseWorkspace();
          } catch {
            // retain for retry
          }
        }
        if (errors.length) {
          throw new AggregateError(errors, "Workspace process cleanup failed");
        }
        this.owners.delete(owner.key);
```

And keep the owner registered only for the processes whose cleanup actually failed.

## Regression test

Register two processes, make one `cleanup()` reject with `uncertain`, call `stopEnvironment`, and assert `activeWorkspaceOperations` reaches 0 and the manifest tree is reclaimable.

## Summary

`mkdtempSync` runs **outside** the `try` that owns the cleanup, and two failure points sit between the `mkdtemp` and the `try`. Each failed load or reload leaves an orphaned `.source-XXXXXX` directory — plus one emitted `.js` per captured source file — inside the managed plugin install directory, in a place the capture sweeper explicitly refuses to reclaim.

## Location

`src/plugins/plugin-source-build.ts:25-38`

```ts
25: export function buildPluginTypeScriptSource(root: string) {
26:   const directory = fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(path.dirname(root), ".source-"));
...
34:   const jiti = createJiti(root, { fsCache: false, moduleCache: false, tsconfigPaths: false });
35:   const { jsx, transform } = jiti.options;
36:   if (!transform) {
37:     throw new Error("Jiti source transformer is unavailable");
38:   }
```

`src/plugins/plugin-source-build.ts:63-67` and the tail:

```ts
63:   const dispose = () => {
64:     disposed = true;
65:     fs.rmSync(directory, { recursive: true, force: true });
66:   };
67:   try {
...
  } catch (error) {
    dispose();
    throw error;
  }
```

## Mechanism

1. `mkdtempSync` at line 26 is **outside** the `try` that owns `dispose()`.
2. Both failure points between 26 and 67 — `createJiti(root, ...)` throwing (line 34) and the explicit `throw` at line 37 — escape **before** `dispose` is ever defined or invoked.
3. The `catch` at the end of the function only covers line 67 onward.
4. The caller keeps no handle to the directory, so nothing can clean it up later.

`prepareSource` in `src/plugins/plugin-instance-module-loader.ts` calls `buildPluginTypeScriptSource(root)` lazily during module resolution, keyed per module root.

## Why the existing sweeper cannot help

`path.dirname(root)` is inside the managed plugin install directory, which is precisely where `pluginSourceCaptureMaintenance` sweeps. But those directories carry no `SQLITE_STAGING_TOKEN_FILES[0]` owner file, and `reclaimInstance` requires it (`src/plugins/plugin-source-capture-directory.ts:127-140`) — so the maintenance sweep **explicitly refuses them**.

## Impact

Disk exhaustion over repeated plugin load/reload attempts. One orphaned directory (plus one `.js` per captured source file) per failed attempt, in a location that neither the capture sweeper nor `dispose` can ever reclaim.

This is the disk side of the same family as the already-reported catalog/plugin build temp leaks, but a **distinct** code path and a **distinct** unreclaimable location.

## Reproduction

Point `plugins.load.paths` at a plugin root that resolves but fails `createJiti` (e.g. a root whose `package.json`/`tsconfig` is malformed enough to make jiti's option resolution throw), then:

```bash
openclaw plugins reload <id>   # repeat N times
```

Each attempt leaves one `.source-XXXXXX` directory.

## Proposed fix

Move the `mkdtempSync` inside the `try`:

```ts
25: export function buildPluginTypeScriptSource(root: string) {
     const directory = fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(path.dirname(root), ".source-"));
     try {
       const jiti = createJiti(root, { fsCache: false, moduleCache: false, tsconfigPaths: false });
       const { jsx, transform } = jiti.options;
       if (!transform) {
         throw new Error("Jiti source transformer is unavailable");
       }
       ...
     } catch (error) {
       fs.rmSync(directory, { recursive: true, force: true });
       throw error;
     }
   }
```

Then delete the now-redundant `dispose()`-based catch.

Additionally, teach the sweeper about this directory shape so pre-existing orphans get reclaimed:

```ts
// pluginSourceCaptureMaintenance: also reclaim .source-* dirs with no owner token
if (entry.name.startsWith(".source-") && entry.isDirectory()) {
  fs.rmSync(path.join(dir, entry.name), { recursive: true, force: true });
}
```

## Regression test

Force `createJiti` to throw, call `buildPluginTypeScriptSource`, and assert no `.source-*` directory remains in `path.dirname(root)`.

## Summary

The outer loop runs from `start - 1` down to `0`, decrementing `start` only when it finds rows sharing a `seq` with the page. In the common case — no seq-group straddling the boundary — `start` never moves, so the inner `while` drains once and the outer loop **still iterates the entire prefix**, coercing `seq` on every projected message. That is O(total) work per page request, on the request's critical path.

## Location

`src/gateway/session-history-snapshot.ts:148-167`

```ts
  let start = typeof limit === "number" && limit > 0 ? Math.max(0, endExclusive - limit) : 0;
  if (start > 0) {
    const pageSeqs = new Set<number>();
    let indexedStart = endExclusive;
    for (let index = start - 1; index >= 0; index--) {
      while (indexedStart > start) {
        const pageSeq = resolveMessageSeq(messages[--indexedStart]);
        if (pageSeq !== undefined) {
          pageSeqs.add(pageSeq);
        }
      }
      const seq = resolveMessageSeq(messages[index]);
      if (seq !== undefined && pageSeqs.has(seq)) {
        start = index;
      }
    }
  }
```

## Mechanism

1. `pageSeqs` is seeded from the page window by the inner `while`, then the outer loop scans backwards looking for a row whose `seq` is in that set.
2. The intent is to extend `start` backwards to include any earlier member of a straddling seq-group.
3. But seq-groups are **contiguous**. Once a scan position's `seq` is not in `pageSeqs` **and** is below the minimum page seq, no earlier row can match — yet the loop keeps going to index 0 regardless.
4. `resolveMessageSeq` does an `asOptionalRecord` + `asPositiveSafeInteger` coercion per call, so the cost per iteration is not trivial.

## Impact

CPU amplification on paged history reads. A client walking backwards through a long history multiplies this cost per page.

Bounded rather than unbounded: the byte budget `getMaxChatHistoryMessagesBytes()` (6 MiB, `server-constants.ts:11-13`) caps the working set. So this is a bounded-but-superlinear cost, not a DoS.

## Reproduction

Repeated `GET /sessions/<key>/history?limit=1000&cursor=<seq>` over a session with many thousands of projected messages. Observe per-request CPU growing with total history rather than staying proportional to the page.

## Proposed fix

Track the minimum page seq and stop as soon as the scan drops below it:

```ts
  if (start > 0) {
    const pageSeqs = new Set<number>();
    let lowestPageSeq = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
    let indexedStart = endExclusive;
    for (let index = start - 1; index >= 0; index--) {
      while (indexedStart > start) {
        const pageSeq = resolveMessageSeq(messages[--indexedStart]);
        if (pageSeq !== undefined) {
          pageSeqs.add(pageSeq);
          lowestPageSeq = Math.min(lowestPageSeq, pageSeq);
        }
      }
      const seq = resolveMessageSeq(messages[index]);
      if (seq !== undefined && seq < lowestPageSeq) {
        break;   // seq groups are contiguous; nothing further back can match
      }
      if (seq !== undefined && pageSeqs.has(seq)) {
        start = index;
        lowestPageSeq = Math.min(lowestPageSeq, seq);
      }
    }
  }
```

## Regression test

Build 50,000 messages with unique seqs and page with `limit=1000`; assert the number of `resolveMessageSeq` calls is bounded by the page size plus the straddled group, not the total.

## Summary

Of the four workspace bootstrap files OpenClaw injects into the system prompt (`SOUL.md`, `MEMORY.md`, `USER.md`, `AGENTS.md`), three get an explicit compliance-priming sentence telling the model how authoritative that file is. `AGENTS.md` — the file specifically meant to hold the agent's own operating rules — gets none. It is injected with the exact same generic treatment as any other file that happens to live in the workspace.

We'd like this to become configurable: an explicit option for whether (and how strongly) `AGENTS.md` is framed as binding instructions versus plain background context, so users relying on smaller/local models — where this gap is most visible — can opt into stronger framing without waiting for a new default to roll out broadly.

## Where we found it

`buildProjectContextSection` (currently `dist/system-prompt-params-B645JcX8.js` in `openclaw@2026.9.1`, function starts around line 234):

```js
function buildProjectContextSection(files) {
  if (files.length === 0) return [];
  const lines = ["# Project Context", ""];
  const hasSoulFile = files.some((file) => getContextFileBasename(file.path) === "soul.md");
  const hasMemoryFile = files.some((file) => getContextFileBasename(file.path) === "memory.md");
  const hasUserFile = files.some((file) => getContextFileBasename(file.path) === "user.md");
  lines.push("Loaded project context:");
  if (hasSoulFile) lines.push("SOUL.md: persona/tone. Follow it unless higher-priority instructions override.");
  if (hasMemoryFile) lines.push("MEMORY.md: durable non-profile facts and decisions; use when relevant unless higher-priority instructions override.");
  if (hasUserFile) lines.push("USER.md: durable user preferences and profile directives; follow unless higher-priority instructions override.");
  lines.push("");
  for (const file of files) lines.push(`## ${file.path}`, "", sanitizeContextFileContentForPrompt(file.content), "");
  return lines;
}
```

There is no `hasAgentsFile` check and no corresponding priming line. `AGENTS.md`'s content lands in the loop at the bottom with nothing but a bare `## <path>` heading — the same treatment a stray README would get if it happened to be in the workspace.

We checked whether a stronger framing exists elsewhere and is only conditionally applied (e.g. gated behind a config flag we hadn't set) before filing this. It does, partially: `readPostCompactionContext` in `builtin-openclaw-DSKrNRlA.js` re-injects named `AGENTS.md` sections after compaction with explicit language ("Critical rules from AGENTS.md:"), but that path is opt-in via `agents.defaults.compaction.postCompactionSections` (schema doc: *"Leave unset or set [] to disable reinjection... enable the legacy default pair"* — implying this used to be default behavior at some point) and only fires post-compaction, never on a session's first turn. We found no other code path that primes `AGENTS.md` on the normal per-turn system prompt build.

## Why we think this matters in practice

We're running a small local model (9B, via `llama-cpp`/`openai-completions`) as the agent's model. Across several sessions we captured directly from the session-history SQLite store, the model:

- Repeatedly used `2>&1`/`command 2>/dev/null` shell redirects that `AGENTS.md`'s `### exec` section explicitly says never to use (the exec tool already separates stdout/stderr; a redirect makes the command structurally unbindable for approval — see the "Unanalyzable risk" mechanism in `exec-approvals-allowlist-*.js` / `system-run-approval-binding-*.js`, which denies such commands outright with **no** human-approval fallback, regardless of permission mode).
- Ignored the same rule again in a follow-up session after we had the model itself rewrite `AGENTS.md` end-to-end for clarity (at the user's request, expanding nearly every section with explicit decision flows and definitions) — ruling out "the rule was ambiguous" as the explanation. The rule is short, unambiguous, and present verbatim.
- Confirmed `AGENTS.md` was well within `bootstrapMaxChars`/`bootstrapTotalMaxChars` at the time (~15k of a 20k per-file budget, ~19k of a 60k total budget) — so this isn't truncation eating the rule either (a related but distinct problem already tracked in #75187).

## What we tested (local patch, not proposing this exact wording as the fix)

Before filing, we patched `buildProjectContextSection` locally to add a `hasAgentsFile` check mirroring the existing three, with this line:

```js
if (hasAgentsFile) lines.push("AGENTS.md: mandatory operating rules for this workspace. These are binding instructions, not background information — follow them exactly, including every constraint on tool usage, unless a higher-priority instruction explicitly overrides a specific rule.");
```

Result across 3 fresh sessions on the patched build: 2 of 3 tasks that previously triggered `2>&1`/`&&`-chaining violations came back completely clean. The third (a more compound request pushing the model toward defensive `cmd 2>/dev/null || fallback` chains) still showed violations, though the model's own reasoning explicitly referenced "run commands separately, approve the safe ones" before partially failing to apply it — a qualitatively different failure mode than the pre-patch sessions, which never showed that self-awareness at all.

So: a real but partial effect from this one framing sentence, on a small sample. We're not claiming this specific wording is the right fix — just that the underlying gap (zero framing at all) is real and reproducible, and that adding *some* framing measurably changes behavior for at least a meaningful subset of cases.

## Request

Add an explicit, documented option for how `AGENTS.md` is framed when injected into the system prompt — at minimum, parity with the treatment `SOUL.md`/`MEMORY.md`/`USER.md` already get (a `hasAgentsFile` priming line), ideally configurable in strength (off / parity with the other three / stronger "binding instructions" wording) so users on smaller or local models can opt into more explicit framing without it becoming a forced default for everyone.

## Environment

- OpenClaw 2026.9.1
- Model: local `llama-cpp` (`openai-completions` API), 9B-class
- Permission mode: workspace (LLM review + human fallback)

prs.body:
On Discord, clicking any button on an `ask_question` / approval card resolves to the wrong option — in practice **every approval is rejected even when the user clicks Approve**. The bug is in the raw HTTP-interaction (webhook) path of the Chat SDK bridge, which decodes `custom_id` by splitting on `:` only and never strips the `\n` delimiter that `@chat-adapter/discord` inserts.

Verified live on a self-hosted v2 install; the fix below resolves it.

## Root cause

Question buttons are built with an index-based encoding (`src/channels/chat-sdk-bridge.ts:494`):

```ts
Button({ id: `ncq:${questionId}:${idx}`, label: opt.label, value: String(idx), style: opt.style })
```

`@chat-adapter/discord`'s `encodeDiscordCustomId(actionId, value)` joins the two with a **newline**:

```
custom_id = `${actionId}\n${value}` = `ncq:<questionId>:<idx>\n<idx>`
```

The webhook handler parses `custom_id` by splitting on `:` only (`chat-sdk-bridge.ts:709-714`):

```ts
if (customId?.startsWith('ncq:')) {
  const colonIdx = customId.indexOf(':', 4); // after "ncq:"
  if (colonIdx !== -1) {
    questionId = customId.slice(4, colonIdx);
    tail = customId.slice(colonIdx + 1);   // becomes "<idx>\n<idx>", e.g. "0\n0"
  }
}
```

`tail` still contains the `\n<idx>` suffix. `resolveSelectedOption` (`chat-sdk-bridge.ts:108-119`) then tries to map an integer index back to the option value, but the numeric guard fails on the embedded newline:

```ts
const candidate = eventValue ?? tail ?? '';
if (render && /^\d+$/.test(candidate)) {   // /^\d+$/.test("0\n0") === false
  const idx = Number(candidate);
  if (render.options[idx]) return render.options[idx].value;
}
return candidate;                          // returns the literal "0\n0"
```

So `selectedOption` becomes `"0\n0"`, matching no option. For approvals the decision is a strict `if (selectedOption !== 'approve') finalizeReject(...)`, so **Approve is rejected**.

**Log fingerprint:** `unknown response value value="0\n0"` and repeated `Approval rejected ... withReason=false`.

## Why the Gateway path hides this

The Discord **Gateway** `onAction` path (`chat-sdk-bridge.ts:333-345`) is correct — the adapter has already decoded the `\n`, so it passes a clean `event.value` (`"0"`) into `resolveSelectedOption`, the numeric guard passes, and the index maps back to `"approve"`. The bug only surfaces when interactions arrive over the **HTTP webhook fallback** (e.g. when the Gateway listener is down, such as an app that hasn't enabled the Message Content privileged intent the adapter requests). The webhook path should decode identically to the Gateway path.

## Fix

Strip the `\n` delimiter from `tail` before resolving:

```ts
if (colonIdx !== -1) {
  questionId = customId.slice(4, colonIdx);
  tail = customId.slice(colonIdx + 1);
  // custom_id from @chat-adapter/discord is `${actionId}\n${value}`
  // (encodeDiscordCustomId). Splitting on ':' leaves the trailing
  // `\n<value>`; strip it so the index resolves like the Gateway path.
  const nlIdx = tail.indexOf('\n');
  if (nlIdx !== -1) tail = tail.slice(0, nlIdx);
}
```

After the strip, `tail` is `"0"`, the numeric guard passes, and `resolveSelectedOption` returns `render.options[0].value` (`"approve"`). Verified on a live install: Approve now resolves to approve and the approval executes.

## Testing

- Reproduced on a live Discord install: every approval-card click rejected; logs showed `value="0\n0"`.
- With the fix applied, `pnpm run build` clean, service restarted — clicking Approve now approves and executes; clicking Reject rejects. No change to the Gateway path.

issues.body:
### Which RFC trigger does this meet?

Architecture: a cross-cutting refactor changing ownership or contracts across established boundaries

### Problem

A2A spec §3.1 defines the binding-independent core operations. ZeroClaw's inbound implementation currently covers only the synchronous form of `SendMessage`:

| Spec operation | Current implementation |
|---|---|
| `SendMessage` | ✅ Runs one turn and returns a `COMPLETED` `Task` |
| `SendStreamingMessage` | ❌ `-32601 MethodNotFound` |
| `GetTask` | ❌ `-32601 MethodNotFound` |
| `ListTasks` | ❌ `-32601 MethodNotFound` |
| `CancelTask` | ❌ `-32601 MethodNotFound` |
| `SubscribeToTask` | ❌ `-32601 MethodNotFound` |

Consequences:

1. **The task lifecycle is unavailable (a missing capability, not an error-shape problem).** The server has no task identity, state machine, or task store, so `GetTask` / `ListTasks` / `CancelTask` have nothing to build on. These three are not capability-gated: they are expected of any server that returns a `Task`. Changing `MethodNotFound` to `-32004` does not supply them.
2. **Asynchronous and multi-turn semantics cannot be expressed.** `returnImmediately`, `taskId` continuation, and `contextId` multi-turn context are all explicitly rejected today, so callers can only make single-turn blocking calls (§3.2.2, §3.4).
3. **Streaming is missing, and the rejection shape is also non-conforming.** The primary problem is that the server produces no intermediate events, so `SendStreamingMessage` / `SubscribeToTask` cannot be implemented; on top of that, when the Agent Card declares `capabilities.streaming: false`, §3.3.4 requires `UnsupportedOperationError`, but the current server falls through to `MethodNotFound`. The former needs a new capability; the latter is only an error shape — this proposal fixes both, but the capability is the substance.
4. **Interoperability is limited.** Other A2A implementations provide the full set of task operations per §3.1; ZeroClaw can only participate in synchronous single-turn scenarios.

**Concrete scenarios:** when delegating a long-running task across instances, a caller cannot obtain a `taskId` first and then poll for progress — it must block until the whole turn completes. Once a task starts it cannot be cancelled. Multi-turn follow-ups must create a new session each time instead of continuing via `contextId`. These are behaviours A2A clients built to the spec rely on directly.

RFC #11254 recorded streaming and an inbound task store as separate capability extensions in its Non-goals; this RFC is the decision for that extension.

### Proposal

Following A2A spec §3.1, add inbound task lifecycle and streaming to the A2A protocol layer (after #11712, `crates/zeroclaw-a2a`):

- **All six message/task core operations become available:** `SendMessage`, `SendStreamingMessage`, `GetTask`, `ListTasks`, `CancelTask`, `SubscribeToTask`.
- **Introduce a task state machine and task store:** `taskId` is always server-generated; `contextId` is generated when absent and accepted and bound to that session when the client supplies it; `returnImmediately`, `taskId` continuation, and `contextId` multi-turn all work; tasks can be queried, listed, and cancelled.
- **Implement both streaming methods together:** they are gated by the same `capabilities.streaming` flag (§3.3.4), so declaring the capability is a commitment — no partial support.
- **Host adaptation:** the gateway connects existing `turn_streamed` events to the A2A event stream and `CancelTask` to its existing cancellation mechanism.
- **Capability advertisement:** the per-agent card sets `capabilities.streaming` to `true`; the catalog card is not a runnable agent and stays `false`.

**Out of scope:** outbound streaming (a client consuming `SendStreamingMessage`, and the streaming output form of `a2a_send`), to be handled by a separate issue/PR.

### Design sketch

**Affected surfaces**

| Location | Change |
|---|---|
| `crates/zeroclaw-a2a/src/a2a/a2a_wire.rs` | New streaming event and `ListTasks` types |
| `crates/zeroclaw-a2a/src/a2a_server/` | State machine, task store, execution orchestration, JSON-RPC methods, SSE route |
| `crates/zeroclaw-gateway/src/a2a.rs` + gateway session layer | Executor and cancellation adaptation; capability advertisement in card construction |
| Operator docs | New methods and their streaming behaviour |

The protocol layer gains no dependency on `zeroclaw-config` / `zeroclaw-runtime`. New dependencies are `zeroclaw-spawn` (the repo bans bare `tokio::spawn`) and `chrono` (`ListTasks` time filtering and ordering; already used in the workspace).

**Interface shape**

```rust
#[non_exhaustive]
pub struct ExecutorContext {
    pub message: Option<Message>,   // None for cancel
    pub task_id: String,
    pub context_id: String,
    pub stored_task: Option<Task>,  // snapshot for continuation / cancel
}

/// The executor's event stream. `Err` ends the stream (internal or transport
/// failure); business failures belong in a `FAILED` terminal event so they
/// travel through the task state machine.
pub type EventStream = BoxStream<'static, Result<StreamResponse, ServerError>>;

pub trait A2aExecutor: Send + Sync {
    /// One execution round. The stream should end with a terminal event;
    /// a non-terminal pause (INPUT_REQUIRED / AUTH_REQUIRED) may end it too.
    fn execute(&self, ctx: ExecutorContext) -> EventStream;

    /// Cancellation events. The default emits a single CANCELED status update.
    /// The protocol layer has already moved the task to CANCELED before this
    /// runs (see D3); the stream can add explanatory events but cannot
    /// authorise a different state.
    fn cancel(&self, ctx: ExecutorContext) -> EventStream {
        let task_id = ctx.task_id.clone();
        let context_id = ctx.context_id.clone();
        Box::pin(stream::once(async move {
            Ok(status_update(&task_id, &context_id, TaskState::TaskStateCanceled))
        }))
    }
}

/// Task version counter (concurrency bookkeeping).
pub type TaskVersion = u64;

/// Storage failures.
/// `NotFound` → -32001, `NotCancelable` → -32002, `InvalidParams` → -32602,
/// `AlreadyExists` / `Internal` → -32603.
pub enum TaskStoreError {
    NotFound(String),
    AlreadyExists(String),
    NotCancelable(String),
    InvalidParams(String),
    Internal(String),
}

/// Retention (TTL / capacity eviction) is the implementation's responsibility:
/// the in-memory default is bounded by `TaskStoreConfig`, a persistent
/// implementation may choose its own policy, and neither may return an evicted
/// task. Non-terminal tasks are never evicted.
#[async_trait]
pub trait TaskStore: Send + Sync + 'static {
    async fn create(&self, alias: &str, task: Task) -> Result<TaskVersion, TaskStoreError>;
    async fn update(&self, alias: &str, task: Task) -> Result<TaskVersion, TaskStoreError>;
    async fn get(&self, alias: &str, task_id: &str) -> Result<Option<Task>, TaskStoreError>;

    /// List tasks for one agent. Takes the protocol's own request/response
    /// types: the handler normalises `pageSize` to the spec bounds, and the
    /// store owns ordering (last update descending), cursor encoding, and
    /// filling `pageSize` / `totalSize`.
    async fn list(
        &self,
        alias: &str,
        req: &ListTasksRequest,
    ) -> Result<ListTasksResponse, TaskStoreError>;

    /// Atomically "check non-terminal + transition to CANCELED", returning the
    /// updated task. Missing → `NotFound`; already terminal → `NotCancelable`
    /// (the handler turns an already-CANCELED task into an idempotent success).
    /// Deliberately has no default implementation: read-then-write is exactly
    /// the check-then-act race this method exists to close.
    async fn begin_cancel(&self, alias: &str, task_id: &str)
        -> Result<Task, TaskStoreError>;
}
```

The storage interface deliberately reuses the protocol's `ListTasksRequest` /
`ListTasksResponse` types. Splitting them into a separate query type would add
a translation layer that has to be kept in sync with the wire shape for no
behavioural gain; a host implementing persistence can deserialize the same
type. The handler still owns what is genuinely protocol logic: clamping
`pageSize` to the spec bounds and applying response shaping
(`includeArtifacts` / `historyLength`).

**One wire-type adjustment:** `Task.artifacts` and `Task.history` are currently `Vec<…>` and always serialize as `[]`, which cannot express "omit the field entirely" when `includeArtifacts=false` (spec §3.1.4 MUST). Both fields gain `skip_serializing_if = "Vec::is_empty"` and keep accepting an absent or `null` value as empty. That satisfies the omission rule while preserving every existing response shape — a task that produced artifacts never has an empty list, so the omission is unambiguous.

**Decisions this RFC asks to settle**

1. **The executor becomes an event-stream contract, for both execution and cancellation.** There is exactly one delivery shape; for non-streaming requests the protocol layer drains the stream to a terminal or interrupted state, and a blocking `SendMessage` returns at either. `cancel` returns an event stream as well, so an executor can attach explanatory events to a cancellation. The state transition itself is **not** the executor's to make: the protocol layer performs the atomic `begin_cancel` transition first (D3) and only then runs the cancellation stream. Business code can describe the cancellation; it cannot authorise a state the protocol layer did not choose.

   **A round must end in a terminal or interrupted state.** An executor that ends its stream while the task is in any other non-terminal state (`SUBMITTED`, `WORKING`) has violated the contract; the server transitions the task to `FAILED` and closes the round. The alternative — leaving it `WORKING` forever — would also exempt it from TTL eviction and report work that can never progress. An executor that wants to pause must emit `INPUT_REQUIRED` or `AUTH_REQUIRED`.
2. **The task store is defined by the protocol layer and replaceable by the host.** The protocol layer provides the trait and a **bounded** in-memory default; a host may substitute a persistent implementation. The storage interface takes the protocol's own `ListTasksRequest` / `ListTasksResponse`: the handler normalises `pageSize` to the spec bounds, and the store owns ordering (last update descending), cursor encoding, and filling `pageSize` / `totalSize`. Response shaping (`includeArtifacts` / `historyLength`) stays in the handler. Rationale: the protocol layer must not depend on host config; unbounded storage is a clear DoS surface; a second query type would duplicate the wire shape for no behavioural gain.
3. **Execution events have one writer; cancellation enters through an atomic store transition.** Execution events for each task are written to the store by exactly one drive loop; `CancelTask` is a second writer, so "only one writer" cannot be assumed. Cancellation therefore runs in two ordered steps: `TaskStore::begin_cancel` atomically performs "check non-terminal + transition to CANCELED", then the executor's `cancel` stream runs and its events are applied and published (it cannot move the task to another state). Repeated cancellation of an already-`CANCELED` task returns that task idempotently; other terminal states return `-32002`. No general CAS/versioning; multi-instance shared writes remain out of scope for v1 — see Decision and revisit surface item 4.
4. **Subscription channels are held per `task_id` and span multiple execution rounds.** When a task pauses at `INPUT_REQUIRED` / `AUTH_REQUIRED`, the drive loop has already finished; if the channel were destroyed with a single execution, `SubscribeToTask` could only emit the first frame, and events produced by a later continuation would never reach existing subscribers. Therefore: channels are held by the `ExecutionManager` keyed by `task_id`; **a continuation reuses the same channel** rather than creating a new one; the channel is cleaned up on terminal state or TTL expiry. This is a direct requirement of §3.1.6 ("can be used with any task that is not in a terminal state").

   **Non-terminal task with no active execution** (after a persistent-store restart, or a task paused at an interrupted state with no process holding it): `SubscribeToTask` must not return `TaskNotFound` — the task exists and is non-terminal. The behaviour is: emit the current `Task` snapshot, then **lazily create** a channel for that `task_id` and keep the stream open; a later `taskId` continuation delivers into the same channel. If no continuation arrives within the TTL, the channel is cleaned up with the task and the stream closes. This path consumes a concurrent-stream slot and is bounded by `max_concurrent_streams`.

   **One execution round at a time.** The channel outliving a round must not allow two rounds to run on one task. A `SendMessage` carrying a `taskId` whose round is still running is rejected with `UnsupportedOperationError` (`-32004`); a continuation is admitted only once the previous round has ended, which in practice means the task is in an interrupted state (`INPUT_REQUIRED` / `AUTH_REQUIRED`). Two concurrent drive loops would write the same stored task from independent snapshots and interleave their events, violating §3.5.2's generated-order requirement. Subscribing is not a round: `SubscribeToTask` attaches to the channel without claiming the round slot.
5. **Identity and isolation.** Storage is scoped by `(alias, task_id)`; `ListTasks` only enumerates the current alias; a non-empty `tenant` is rejected with `InvalidParams`; when the gateway maps `contextId` into a session key it must bind the alias and constrain length/character set.
6. **`SendMessage` always returns a `Task`, never a bare `Message`.** The spec allows either; the current business path is naturally one agent turn, so maintaining two response paths is unnecessary. This implies a constraint: a `StreamResponse::Message` event inside the executor stream is **ignored and logged at WARN** — the server has already created a Task, and returning a Message as well would satisfy both branches of the `oneof`.
7. **Configuration ownership stays with the host, and v1 exposes no new keys.** The protocol layer defines `TaskStoreConfig` and `ExecutionManager::with_max_streams` with **bounded defaults** (`max_tasks = 1000` per alias, `task_ttl_secs = 3600`, `max_concurrent_streams = 64`). A host that needs different bounds constructs the store and manager with its own values. Adding `[a2a.server]` keys is deferred until a deployment actually needs them: a published config key is a durable compatibility promise, and the defaults already close the DoS surface.

**Spec conformance**

Field shapes, error-code mappings, pagination and ordering, `historyLength`, `includeArtifacts`, `A2A-Version` negotiation, and the SSE frame format (§9.4.2: each frame is a complete JSON-RPC response envelope) all follow the spec and are not restated here.

A few spec details that are easy to miss in implementation:

- Every state transition writes `TaskStatus.timestamp` (`ListTasks` ordering and `statusTimestampAfter` filtering depend on it).
- `ListTasks` is ordered by last update time **descending** with cursor pagination; `pageSize` defaults to 50 and caps at 100, with oversized requests clamped rather than rejected; `nextPageToken` is always present and is the empty string on the last page.
- `historyLength` applies both to `GetTask` and to the `Task` returned inline by `SendMessage`.
- The first frame of a stream must be a `Task` (or the whole stream is a single `Message`, which this proposal does not support); the stream closes immediately after a terminal event.
- `SubscribeToTask` returns `-32004` for a terminal task; for a non-terminal task (including `INPUT_REQUIRED` / `AUTH_REQUIRED`) the stream **does not end** — it stays open across continuation rounds until the task reaches a terminal state.

Two internal tensions in the spec text, with the interpretation this proposal adopts:

| Tension | Interpretation |
|---|---|
| §3.1.1 "MUST return immediately with either task information or response message" vs §3.2.2 requiring `returnImmediately=false` to block until terminal/interrupted | §3.1.1 constrains the response shape (Task or Message, not a long-lived connection); §3.2.2 constrains timing. Follow §3.2.2. |
| §3.3.1 calls cancellation idempotent vs §3.1.5 requiring `TaskNotCancelableError` for terminal tasks | Already-`CANCELED` → return the task idempotently ("repeating a cancel has no side effects"); `COMPLETED` / `FAILED` / `REJECTED` → `-32002`. |

**Basis for the design**

This proposal takes **A2A spec v1.0.1** as its authority. `a2a-rs` v0.5.3 is used only as a reference implementation, to cross-check spec details and to surface pitfalls; where it disagrees with the spec, the spec wins.

| Basis | Content |
|---|---|
| **Spec-mandated** | The parameter/return shapes of the six methods; error codes; SSE frames as complete JSON-RPC envelopes; paging 50/100; `ListTasks` ordered by update time descending with a cursor; `nextPageToken` always present; `statusTimestampAfter` inclusive; terminal-task continuation `-32004`; undeclared capability `-32004`; terminal cancel `-32002`; repeat-cancel idempotent |
| **Not specified — this proposal's choice** | Executor contract as an event stream for both execution and cancellation, with the state transition owned by the protocol layer and a non-terminal stream end treated as `FAILED` (D1/D3); storage interface using the protocol's `ListTasksRequest` / `ListTasksResponse` (D2); subscription channel held across rounds, with one round per task at a time (D4); non-empty `tenant` rejected on every method (D5); return only `Task` (D6); config types defined by the protocol layer, values supplied by the host (D7) |
| **Reference-implementation choices not adopted** | Unbounded in-memory store without TTL or eviction; built-in `list` ordered by task id ascending with offset paging and no `pageSize` / `totalSize` in the response (the spec requires update-time descending, a cursor, and all three fields); `includeArtifacts` parsed only as a REST query parameter, never applied as field omission |
| **Reference-implementation behaviour corrected per spec** | Removing the active execution as soon as a run finishes, which makes `SubscribeToTask` return 404 for an interrupted task — the spec requires any non-terminal task to be subscribable |

Where the reference implementation agrees with the spec (SSE envelopes, page limits, capability gating, terminal-state semantics), it is not restated; implement per the spec.

**Implementation split**

One PR, submitted as independently reviewable layers: wire types → task store → execution orchestration → JSON-RPC/SSE handler → gateway adaptation.

### Alternatives considered

- **Ship a synchronous task store first, then streaming separately.** The executor contract and drive loop would be rewritten wholesale in the second step, the same files would be reviewed twice, and an intermediate state of a public trait would be left behind. The crate is not yet published to crates.io, which makes this the moment to settle it.
- **Reuse an existing third-party implementation's frame format and storage shape.** Some A2A implementations emit bare `StreamResponse` frames, order and paginate `ListTasks` in ways not fully aligned with §3.1.4, and use unbounded in-memory stores. Copying would import spec deviations; this proposal follows the spec text.
- **Adopt an existing A2A Rust SDK server wholesale and replace the current inbound path.** A full replacement would indeed avoid "two wire models", but the existing wire and protocol handling already include repo-specific hardening (SSRF domain pinning, response-body caps, untrusted-content fencing), so replacement is an order of magnitude larger and beyond "close a capability gap". Whether to migrate to a third-party SDK should be a separate issue.
- **Do task management only, leaving streaming declared as `false`.** Conformant, but it defers streaming entirely; adding it later would still touch the same handler and execution orchestration.
- **Do nothing.** The core operations remain missing and there is nowhere for asynchronous or multi-turn semantics to live.

### Non-goals

- Outbound streaming (a client consuming SSE; the streaming output form of `a2a_send`)
- Push notification methods
- A persistent `TaskStore` implementation (only the trait and an in-memory default)
- Bare `Message` responses
- `GetExtendedAgentCard`
- gRPC / REST bindings (JSON-RPC over HTTP only)
- Cross-alias task aggregation and multi-tenant authorization
- Idempotency de-duplication based on `messageId`

### Risks and mitigations

- **Unbounded task-store growth.** A bounded default in the protocol layer: per-alias `max_tasks` plus a terminal-task TTL; eviction only drops terminal tasks with no subscribers.
- **SSE long connections exhausting resources.** A concurrent-stream cap on the execution manager, keep-alive, and terminating plus logging when a subscriber lags.
- **New injection surface bypassing authorization.** Admission runs before the SSE connection is established; the standalone router stays closed by default.
- **Cancel/terminal race.** The cancel path uses the atomic `begin_cancel` transition; the drive loop checks the snapshot for a terminal state before each event, so a cancelled task cannot later be written as `COMPLETED`.
- **Subscription channel leaks.** Channels are registered per `task_id` and explicitly cleaned up on terminal state, cancellation, or TTL expiry.
- **A non-terminal task with no executor held open by a subscriber.** Channels are created lazily and reclaimed with the task's TTL; the concurrent-stream cap bounds the count; cleanup closes the stream rather than dropping it silently.
- **Gateway session cross-talk.** `contextId` mapping binds the alias and constrains character set and length.
- **Tasks lost on process restart.** The in-memory store is not persistent; after a restart `GetTask` returns `-32001`. Persistence is left to the host implementation.
- **Executor stream ends naturally without a terminal event.** The server logs a WARN and returns the last snapshot to the synchronous request / closes the SSE stream; it must not hang the request. This is the degradation path for an executor violating its contract.
- **Executor emits a `Message` event inside a Task lifecycle.** Ignored and logged at WARN (see D6), so the `SendMessageResponse` oneof never carries both branches.
- **Public trait changes.** The crate is unpublished; the change lands in one PR with the sole implementation and all tests updated.
- **Rollback.** `[a2a.server].enabled` remains the master switch; reverting the single PR restores synchronous behaviour.

### Breaking change?

Potentially - needs investigation

### Decision and revisit surface

Discussion and the vote should settle:

1. **Executor contract:** both `execute` and `cancel` return event streams, with the state transition owned by the protocol layer (`begin_cancel` runs first) — acceptable? This is the point at which the public trait is fixed.
2. **Storage ownership:** `TaskStore` defined by the protocol layer, a bounded in-memory default, replaceable by the host, and taking the protocol's own `ListTasksRequest` / `ListTasksResponse` — consistent with the config-ownership boundary in RFC #11254?
3. **Concurrency and cancellation semantics:** one writer for execution events, at most one execution round per task at a time, cancellation through the atomic `begin_cancel` transition before the executor's cancel stream runs, no general CAS, and no requirement that the host implement `cancel` (cancellation guarantees consistent state, not immediate abortion of underlying work) — acceptable?
4. **Coupling between the concurrency model and multi-instance deployment:** v1 assumes one process advances each task. If a host later moves to a shared persistent store with multiple instances, both `begin_cancel`'s atomicity and the drive loop's writes need cross-process coordination (versioning/CAS or a task-ownership lease). Does this proposal accept recording that constraint explicitly — "persistent implementations are limited to in-process substitution; multi-instance requires a concurrency-contract upgrade first" — rather than doing it now?
5. **Isolation model:** alias scoping and rejecting a non-empty `tenant` — does this fit the current deployment shape?

Once accepted, the implementation PR links this issue with `Implements #<this issue>`.

### Data hygiene checks

- [x] I removed personal/sensitive data from examples, payloads, and logs.

prs.body:
---
name: odt
description: "Use this skill whenever the user wants to create, fill, read, or convert OpenDocument Format files (.odt, .ods). Triggers include: any mention of 'ODT', 'ODS', 'ODF', 'OpenDocument', 'LibreOffice document', or requests to produce documents in open-source or ISO standard formats. Also use when converting HTML, Markdown, or TipTap/ProseMirror JSON to ODT, filling ODT templates with data, reading or extracting content from ODT files, generating ODS spreadsheets, reading ODS files, converting XLSX to ODS, converting DOCX to ODT, or creating formal documents like resolutions, reports, letters, contracts, invoices, or memos as .odt files. Use this skill instead of the docx skill when the user explicitly requests .odt output or mentions LibreOffice/OpenOffice as their target application. Do NOT use for .docx files (use the docx skill), PDFs, presentations, or general coding tasks unrelated to document generation."
license: Apache-2.0
---

# odf-kit — OpenDocument Format for JavaScript and TypeScript

## Overview

An `.odt` file is an OpenDocument Format text document — an ISO standard (ISO/IEC 26300) ZIP archive containing XML files. It is the native format for LibreOffice, Apache OpenOffice, Collabora, OnlyOffice, and is supported by Google Docs and Microsoft Office.

odf-kit is the only actively maintained JavaScript/TypeScript library for ODF generation. It produces spec-validated output (validated against the OASIS ODF Validator on every CI run). Works in Node.js 22+ and browsers. No LibreOffice dependency.

## Quick Reference

| Task | Import | Function |
|------|--------|----------|
| Create new ODT document | `"odf-kit"` | `new OdtDocument()` |
| Fill existing ODT template | `"odf-kit"` | `fillTemplate(bytes, data)` |
| HTML → ODT | `"odf-kit"` | `htmlToOdt(html, options?)` |
| Markdown → ODT | `"odf-kit"` | `markdownToOdt(markdown, options?)` |
| TipTap/ProseMirror JSON → ODT | `"odf-kit"` | `tiptapToOdt(json, options?)` |
| Build ODS spreadsheet | `"odf-kit"` | `new OdsDocument()` |
| Read ODT / convert to HTML | `"odf-kit/reader"` | `readOdt(bytes)` / `odtToHtml(bytes)` |
| Read ODS | `"odf-kit/ods-reader"` | `readOds(bytes)` / `odsToHtml(bytes)` |
| XLSX → ODS | `"odf-kit/xlsx"` | `xlsxToOds(bytes)` |
| DOCX → ODT | `"odf-kit/docx"` | `docxToOdt(bytes, options?)` |
| ODT → Typst / PDF | `"odf-kit/typst"` | `odtToTypst(bytes)` |

### Installation

```bash
npm install odf-kit
```

Requires Node.js 22 or later. ESM only.

---

## Creating New ODT Documents

Generate .odt files with JavaScript using odf-kit's builder API.

### Setup

```javascript
import { OdtDocument } from "odf-kit";
import { writeFileSync } from "fs";

const doc = new OdtDocument();
doc.addHeading("Document Title", 1);
doc.addParagraph("Body text goes here.");

const bytes = await doc.save();
writeFileSync("output.odt", bytes);
```

### Metadata

```javascript
doc.setMetadata({
  title: "Quarterly Report",
  creator: "Jane Doe",
  description: "Q4 2026 financial summary",
});
```

### Page Layout

```javascript
doc.setPageLayout({
  orientation: "landscape",       // or "portrait" (default)
  width: "21cm",                  // default A4
  height: "29.7cm",
  marginTop: "2cm",
  marginBottom: "2cm",
  marginLeft: "2cm",
  marginRight: "2cm",
});
```

**For US Letter:** Use `width: "8.5in"` and `height: "11in"`.

### Headers and Footers

```javascript
// Simple string (### = page number)
doc.setHeader("Confidential — Page ###");
doc.setFooter("© 2026 Acme Corp — Page ###");

// Builder for formatted headers
doc.setHeader((h) => {
  h.addText("Confidential", { bold: true, color: "gray" });
  h.addText(" — Page ");
  h.addPageNumber();
});
```

### Headings

```javascript
// Basic heading (levels 1–6)
doc.addHeading("Chapter Title", 1);
doc.addHeading("Section Title", 2);

// With paragraph options
doc.addHeading("Centered Title", 1, { align: "center" });
doc.addHeading("Title with Space", 1, { spaceBefore: "0.5cm", spaceAfter: "0.5cm" });
```

### Paragraphs

```javascript
// Plain text
doc.addParagraph("Simple paragraph.");

// With paragraph options
doc.addParagraph("Right-aligned text.", { align: "right" });

// Builder for mixed formatting
doc.addParagraph((p) => {
  p.addText("Normal and ");
  p.addText("bold", { bold: true });
  p.addText(" text.");
});

// Builder with paragraph options
doc.addParagraph((p) => {
  p.addText("Indented paragraph content.");
}, { indentLeft: "1cm", indentFirst: "0.5cm" });
```

**Paragraph options:**

| Option | Type | Example | Description |
|--------|------|---------|-------------|
| `align` | string | `"left"`, `"center"`, `"right"`, `"justify"` | Text alignment |
| `spaceBefore` | string | `"0.5cm"` | Space above paragraph |
| `spaceAfter` | string | `"0.5cm"` | Space below paragraph |
| `lineHeight` | number or string | `1.5` or `"150%"` | Line spacing (number = multiplier) |
| `indentLeft` | string | `"1cm"` | Left indent |
| `indentRight` | string | `"1cm"` | Right indent |
| `indentFirst` | string | `"0.5cm"` | First-line indent (positive) or hanging indent (negative) |
| `tabStops` | array | `[{ position: "6cm", type: "right" }]` | Tab stop positions |

### Text Formatting

```javascript
doc.addParagraph((p) => {
  p.addText("Bold", { bold: true });
  p.addText("Italic", { italic: true });
  p.addText("Underline", { underline: true });
  p.addText("Strikethrough", { strikethrough: true });
  p.addText("Super", { superscript: true });
  p.addText("Sub", { subscript: true });
  p.addText("Arial 14pt red", { fontFamily: "Arial", fontSize: 14, color: "#CC0000" });
  p.addText("Highlighted", { highlightColor: "#FFFF00" });
  p.addText("UPPERCASE", { textTransform: "uppercase" });
  p.addText("Small Caps", { smallCaps: true });
  p.addText("Weight 300", { fontWeight: 300 });   // numeric 100–900
  p.addText("Weight bold", { fontWeight: "bold" });
});
```

**All text formatting options:**

| Option | Type | Example |
|--------|------|---------|
